Our eyes perceive the world only in the language of light.

00:34:11.500 --> 00:34:15.580
Yet light, visible light, is but a narrow slice of energy

00:34:15.700 --> 00:34:19.580
contained within an infinite spectrum of electromagnetic waves

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:22.190
that constantly vibrate all around us.

00:34:37.260 --> 00:34:41.450
When scientists analyze light, breaking it apart into its component wavelengths,

00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:45.720
the familiar rainbow of colors from red to violet appears.

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:51.880
Colors are the brain's code for the wavelengths of light we can see.

00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:56.590
Beyond this band of energy, our naked eyes go blind.

00:35:00.920 --> 00:35:05.280
The world around us hides numerous sights from our limited light-sensitive eyes.

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:19.100
By equipping a camera with a sensitive filter, we can see the world reflected in ultraviolet light,

00:35:19.780 --> 00:35:22.760
the invisible wavelengths of energy beyond the color violet.

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:37.990
In the 1930s, scientists discovered that honeybees have a visual sensitivity that extends beyond our own.

00:35:39.140 --> 00:35:44.420
On its daily search for nectar, the bee can sense its surroundings in ultraviolet light.

00:35:47.380 --> 00:35:58.120
Some flowers we see as solidly colored have a very different appearance to the bee.

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:04.120
When viewed in ultraviolet light, new shadings and patterns appear.

00:36:10.670 --> 00:36:13.170
Hoping to guide the bee to nectar and pollen,

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:18.200
ultraviolet markings hidden from our eyes have been discovered on numerous flowers.

00:36:25.940 --> 00:36:29.320
Unseen ultraviolet rays stream abundantly from the sun.

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:34.560
But they are only one kind of invisible light that we must rely on cameras to reveal.

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:59.880
We see the light of a burning match.

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:02.660
but an image of its heat eludes us.

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:07.470
If our eyes could see the part of the spectrum

00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:10.230
where red light turns to infrared, or heat,

00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:14.420
our view of the world would suddenly take on a new and expanded scope.

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:36.800
A technique called Schlieren photography allows us to see heat energy that constantly flows all around us.

00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:52.550
A valuable new tool in medicine, super-sensitive infrared cameras can detect slight variations in skin temperature,

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:56.880
which often signal early warnings of cancerous tumors and other diseases.

00:38:01.310 --> 00:38:04.830
Each color represents a one-half degree difference in temperature.

00:38:05.770 --> 00:38:08.170
Red areas are the warmest, blue the coolest.

00:38:10.210 --> 00:38:18.220
To a doctor's trained eye, the body's varied heat patterns show a wealth of vital diagnostic information once hidden from his view.

00:38:40.970 --> 00:38:46.540
By photographing a subject with visible light, only the outer surface details are recorded by the camera.

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:54.540
Using another form of energy, invisible to the eye, we can penetrate solid matter and create an image on film.

00:39:18.620 --> 00:39:24.740
Discovered in 1895, X-rays were briefly considered by some to be a threat to feminine modesty.

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:29.980
However, fears were laid at first sight of the image, and the X-ray...

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.080
was quickly put to use as a valuable new tool of medicine.

00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:51.720
The power of the X-ray is expanding our knowledge of the past.

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:58.070
When fragile Egyptian mummies are subjected to modern X-ray analysis,

00:39:58.600 --> 00:39:59.960
scientists gain new...

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:10.220
insight into their little known culture and lives. What time and wrappings have hidden,

00:40:10.930 --> 00:40:21.600
x-rays can still reveal. X-rays of Yuya, a royal advisor, show obvious dental disease.

00:40:26.060 --> 00:40:29.960
Fuya, his wife, suffered painfully from arthritis and a badly curved...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:38.730
spine. The infant pediamen received a less than noble burial. His arms were amputated and his legs

00:40:38.730 --> 00:40:49.220
were broken to fit an undersized coffin. An unidentified mummy, a less desirable fate. Legs

00:40:49.220 --> 00:40:59.880
are intact, but the torso is mysteriously missing. Pharaoh Amenhotep I, X-raying directly.

00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:05.140
through his beautifully preserved coffin reveals that his body had been damaged by ancient grave

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:16.320
robbers and repaired by priests five centuries later. Perhaps no pharaoh is better known than

00:41:16.320 --> 00:41:24.790
the young king Tutankhamun. Penetrating rays show that his golden mask was constructed in several

00:41:24.790 --> 00:41:29.970
parts. His beard was added last. Attached.

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:39.830
to the chin by a tapered peg. The body of King Tut itself has undergone careful analysis in hopes

